.SH NAME
ktm-yaml \- Reading and writing YAML documents.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
import yaml;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Reading and writing YAML documents.
.SH TYPES
.SS Reader
.EX
struct Reader
{
    u8[] text;
    bool parsed;
    i64 pos;
    Event *evs;
    i64 nev;
    i64 evcap;
    i64 ev;
    i64 depth;
    i64 cap;
    ReaderLevel *levels;
    i64 pdepth;
    i64 pcap;
    ParseLevel *plevels;
    u8[] k;
    String buf;
    bool strict;
};
.EE
.PP
Reads a YAML document front to back the way json.Reader reads JSON, with the same calls, so one decoder can serve both: open a mapping with beginObject() and loop on nextKey(), open a sequence with beginArray() and loop on nextItem(), and read each value with readString/readInt/readFloat/readBool (isNull() first where null is allowed, skipValue() for one you don't want). Text that isn't YAML, a part of YAML that isn't supported (anchors and aliases, tags, << merge keys, several documents) and a value of the wrong kind throw ERR_DECODE with where it is: "3:14: lines[2].price: expected a number, got a string". Make one with yaml.readerNew(text) (the text is viewed, not copied: keep it alive) and free it with r.free(). Its fields are read-only outside module yaml.
.SS Writer
.EX
struct Writer
{
    String out;
    i64 depth;
    bool done;
    u8 levels[MAX_DEPTH];
};
.EE
.PP
Builds a YAML document in block style into a String: begin/end the mappings and sequences, key() each entry, and add the values. Each level is indented two spaces, a sequence item starts with "- ", and a string is quoted only when it would otherwise read back as something else. An empty mapping or sequence is written {} or []. Make one with yaml.writerNew() (a declared Writer is all zero, not set up), free it with w.free(), and take the text with w.take(). Its fields are read-only outside module yaml.
.SH CONSTANTS
.SS STRING
.EX
0
.EE
.PP
What Reader.peek() says the next value is: the same numbers as json's kinds, so code can serve both. STRING is any quoted or block scalar and any plain one that isn't one of the others; INT an integer that fits an i64; FLOAT any other number (also .inf and .nan); BOOL true or false; NULL null, ~ or nothing at all; ARRAY a sequence; OBJECT a mapping.
.SS FLOAT
.EX
1
